<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-6">
	<h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-ink mb-4">A Rep for Delegation</h3>
	<dl class="flex flex-col gap-4">
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">Cue</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				You observe a mistake in progress on a task you have already delegated.
				Your instinct is to take it back and do it yourself.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">The Story I Tell Myself</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				Why isn't this person taking this as seriously as I do? If this doesn't
				get done right it reflects on me — and that means I'm not as capable or
				in control as I need to be.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">The Preference</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				I hope they take this as seriously as I do, but if they don't, it's not
				the end of the world. My job is to develop people, not to do their work
				for them. I'll give them clear feedback and the opportunity to correct
				it themselves.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">The Rep</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				Instead of taking the task back, have a brief, specific conversation
				with the person about what needs to be corrected and why. Be clear
				about the standard. Then step back and give them 24 hours to work on
				it before you reassess. Do not check in before the 24 hours are up.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">The Anchor</dt>
			<dd class="pull-quote text-ink">
				"Don't be ashamed to need help. Like a soldier storming a wall, you
				have a mission to accomplish. And if you've been wounded and you need
				a comrade to pull you up? So what?" — Marcus Aurelius, Book 7
			</dd>
		</div>
	</dl>
</div>
